# Machines the validation matrix should drive, one configuration per line:
#
#   machine | user@host | target | engine | preprocess | suite | transport (optional)
#
# machine     a short label; configurations sharing it run sequentially, and different
#             labels run in parallel. Two labels must therefore never name the same
#             physical box -- see the dual-boot note at the bottom.
# user@host   SSH destination, or `local` to use this machine
# target      build target (cpu | intel | intel-xpu | nvidia | nvidia-whisperx | full |
#             nvidia-intel | amd | amd-rocm-torch)
# engine      ASR_ENGINE for the run
# preprocess  ASR_PREPROCESS_DEVICE (AUTO | CPU | GPU | NPU | CUDA) -- UVR runs here, and
#             it is independent of the ASR device
# suite       accuracy | smoke | full | stress | longform
# transport   `linux` (default), `wsl`, or `wsl:<distro>`. Windows SSH lands in cmd.exe,
#             so a Windows host must be driven through its WSL2 distro.
#
# Copy this file to .validation-matrix.conf (gitignored) and fill in your own hosts, or
# point VALIDATION_MATRIX_PLAN at it. Depth per configuration follows the tiering in
# docs/REMOTE_VALIDATION.md: one full matrix per machine on its primary engine, the smoke
# set for every other combination, and the long-form clip wherever there is an NVIDIA GPU.
#
# Which engine can use which device is a hard constraint, not a preference:
#   FASTER-WHISPER  CTranslate2  -> CUDA or CPU only; no OpenVINO or ROCm backend exists
#   INTEL-WHISPER   OpenVINO     -> Intel GPU (the NPU cannot execute Whisper's dynamic IR)
#   OPENAI-WHISPER  torch        -> CUDA, Intel XPU (intel-xpu image), or ROCm
#   WHISPERX        torch        -> CUDA
# A combination outside that table would silently run on the CPU and report a clean pass.

# nuc|user@10.0.0.5|intel|INTEL-WHISPER|NPU|full
# nuc|user@10.0.0.5|intel|INTEL-WHISPER|GPU|smoke
# gpubox|user@10.0.0.6|nvidia|FASTER-WHISPER|AUTO|stress
# gpubox|user@10.0.0.6|nvidia|WHISPERX|AUTO|smoke

# A Windows host is driven through WSL2. Only NVIDIA reaches a Linux container there --
# Intel GPU/NPU are not exposed at all, and AMD appears via /dev/dxg but falls back to the
# CPU -- so `nvidia` is the only target that can support an accelerator claim.
# gpubox|user@10.0.0.6|nvidia|FASTER-WHISPER|AUTO|smoke|wsl:Ubuntu

# Dual-boot hosts: retain one physical-machine label across Linux and Windows rows. The
# matrix serializes all rows with that label, preventing both operating systems from
# attempting a concurrent GPU run. Select the booted operating system's rows by keeping
# only those rows in the active plan:
#
# gpubox|user@10.0.0.6|nvidia|FASTER-WHISPER|AUTO|smoke|linux
# gpubox|user@10.0.0.6|nvidia|FASTER-WHISPER|AUTO|smoke|wsl:Ubuntu
